Enhancing the interfacial charge carriers transfer efficiency is important for designing photocatalysts with excellent hydrogen evolution performance. In this work, we have successfully constructed a $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ ternary heterostructure by embedding InN at the interface of thin-layered $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ and tubular $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ derived from metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) nanorods for the first time. The InN can not only adjust the energy band structure of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , but also boost the photogenerated charge carriers transfer at the interface of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $Z\mathrm{nIn}_{2}S_{4}$ . The optimum photocatalytic hydrogen evolution rate of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ composite reaches $275~{\upmu\mathrm{mol/h}}$ $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of catalyst) under simulated sunlight irradiation, which is obviously higher than pure $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ (12.5 times), $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ (2.5 times) and binary $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ (1.8 times) photocatalysts. This work can offer a meaningful strategy to promote the interfacial charge separation in the heterostructure for excellent photocatalytic hydrogen evolution activity.
