## 1. Introduction
Nowadays, the energy crisis and environmental pollution have become two social concerns due to the extensive use of fossil fuels [1]. Hydrogen is considered as an ideal alternative energy source, which has special properties, such as high calorific value, carbonfree attributes and so on [2]. Since Fujishima and Honda firstly reported the electrochemical photolysis of water at a titanium dioxide electrode in 1972 [3], the exploration of excellent catalysts for photocatalytic hydrogen evolution via water-splitting has become a research hotspot in the field of renewable energy conversion $[4-$ 9]. In the past few years, a great number of highly efficient photocatalysts have been reported, such as $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ [10,11], metal oxides [12,13], metal sulfides [14–16], metal nitrides [17,18] and metalorganic-frameworks (MOFs) [19–23]. However, the catalytic performance of single catalyst is largely limited due to its poor light absorption capacity and rapid recombination of the photogenerated charge carriers. Therefore, constructing heterostructure systems is an effective strategy for higher photocatalytic activity [24–27].
$Z\mathrm{nIn}_{2}S_{4}.$ , which has a narrow band gap and a suitable conduction band potential for proton reduction, has been regarded as an ideal photocatalyst [17,28–31]. However, its photocatalytic performance is deeply restricted by the rapid recombination of the photogenerated charge carriers. In recent years, materials derived from MOFs have been used as photocatalysts for water splitting [32–36], which show a lot of special properties. Among them, $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ is a well-known semiconductor derived from In-MOF, which can combine with other nanomaterials as an excellent carrier [37–43]. Various photocatalysts based on MOF-derived $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ have been synthesized, such as $\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}/\mathrm{Bi}_{2}0_{3}$ [37], $\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}/\mathrm{g}{-}C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ [38,39], $\ln_{2}{0_{3}}/\Gamma\mathrm{d}\mathrm{S}$ [40], $\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}/\mathrm{CuO}$ [41], $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ [42], $\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}/\mathrm{CdZnS}$ [43] and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ [44]. However, the detailed photocatalytic mechanism of the composites is still unclear and need to be further studied.
It is known that InN has high electron density and an excellent electron transport property [45], besides, its visible light absorption is much higher than many other semiconductors with a narrow band gap of around $0.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ [46]. Thus, a number of InNbased photocatalysts have been reported, including $\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ [46], $\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ [47] and $\mathrm{InN/GaZnON}$ [48]. However, the construction and the photocatalytic hydrogen evolution performance of $\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}/\mathrm{InN}$ composite have not been reported up to now.
In particular, a MOF-derived $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ heterostructure has been reported in our group previously [44], in which the In-N-In sites can boost the interfacial charge transfer for enhanced photocatalytic hydrogen evolution. However, the detailed effect mechanism of In-N-In sites is still needed to be investigated (pure InN phase cannot be determined, at least in the TEM images). Therefore, in this work, we further explored the effect of crystalline InN phase in the heterostructural composite and a novel ternary photocatalyst $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ was synthesized by facile hydrothermal and calcination methods for the first time. Under sunlight irradiation, the optimum hydrogen evolution rate reaches a value of $275\ \upmu\mathrm{mol/h}$ , when the mass ratio of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ and melamine is set at 1: 20, about 12.5, 2.5 and 1.8 times higher than the pure $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ , $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , respectively. The enhanced photocatalytic activity can be attributed to the formation of heterostructure with InN embedded at the interface of $\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}/$ $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ heterostructure, further accelerating the migration of the interfacial photogenerated charge carriers. Furthermore, this work can provide some new strategies for boosting the interfacial charge carriers transfer in heterostructure composite.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
2-Aminoterephthalic acid $(99\%)$ and zinc chloride $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{nCl}}_{2}$ , $98\%$ ) were purchased from Aldrich. Indium chloride tetrahydrate $(\mathrm{InCl}_{3}.$ $\boldsymbol{4}\mathrm{H}_{2}\boldsymbol{0}$ , $99.9\%$ ) was purchased from Aladdin, and other chemicals of analytical grade were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. All reagents were used without further purification.
## 2.2. Preparation of samples
## 2.2.1. Preparation of In-MOF
The synthesis of In-MOF nanorods refers to our previous work [44]. In detail, $598~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}4.5\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $234~\mathrm{mg}$ of 2- aminoterephthalic acid are added into $20\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\textrm~{~~}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{mL}}$ of N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF) and stirred for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Then, the dispersion is transferred into a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave and kept at $125^{\circ}\mathrm{~\vec{C}~}$ for $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After natural cooling to room temperature, the product is collected by centrifuging and washing with DMF and ethanol for several times, respectively. Finally, the In-MOF sample is obtained after drying overnight at $80~\mathrm{^{\circ}C}$ .
## 2.2.2. Preparation of $I n_{2}O_{3}$
The above In-MOF precursors are put into a crucible and covered with an aluminum foil. Then, the samples are calcined at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ in ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere to get the pure $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ .
## 2.2.3. Preparation of $I n_{2}O_{3}@I n N$
The melamine is used as the source of nitrogen element, and the $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ composites with different InN ratios are firstly synthesized through a facile calcination method. In detail, $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of the above $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ and a certain amount of melamine are mixed in a mortar. After absolute grinding, the mixture is put into a crucible and covered with an aluminum foil. Followed with calcination at 700 $\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ under $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere, the obtained sample is labeled as $\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ . Different mass ratio of the $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ to InN (1:2, 1:5, 1:20, 1:40, 1:60) are prepared by adding different amounts of melamine $(0.1\mathrm{~g},0.25\mathrm{~g},1.0\mathrm{~g},2.0\mathrm{~g},3.0\mathrm{~g})$ . For comparison, 650 and $750^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are also used as calcination temperature.
## 2.2.4. Preparation of $I n_{2}O_{3}@I n N/Z n I n_{2}S_{4}$
The ternary catalysts with different amount of $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ are synthesized by a typical hydrothermal method [44]. In detail, $18~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ is added into $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of the mixed solution of water and glycerol (v $ :\mathrm{~v~}=4:1\$ ), followed with ultrasound treatment for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . Afterwards, $163~\mathrm{mg}$ of $Z\mathrm{nCl}_{2}$ , $351~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{InCl}_{3}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $180\mathrm{mg}$ of thioacetamide (TAA) are added into the above dispersion and stirred for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Then, the mixture is placed and heated in a water bath at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $90\mathrm{min}$ under continuous stirring. After natural cooling, the samples can be collected by centrifuging and washing with deionized water and ethanol for several times, and the products are labeled as $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ after drying at 80 $\mathrm{{}^{\circ}C}$ overnight. For comparison, different mass ratio of $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{In}_{2}0_{3}@\operatorname{InN}}\end{array}$ to $Z\mathrm{nIn}_{2}S_{4}$ (1:1, 1:2, 1:4) are synthesized by adjusting the amount of $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{In}_{2}0_{3}@\operatorname{InN}.}\end{array}$ , and the pure $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ is prepared by a similar process without adding $\ln_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ .
## 2.2.5. Preparation of comparative samples
$\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ is prepared by adding MOF-derived $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ instead of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ . The catalyst synthesized by directly mixing $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}@.$ InN and the $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ is labeled as M-sample.
## 2.3. Characterization
The crystal structures of the samples are measured by X-ray diffraction (XRD) (D2, Bruker Corp., Germany). The morphologies and microstructure of the composites are observed with scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Phenom Prox, Netherlands) and fieldemission transmission electron microscopy (FE-TEM, Tecnai G2 F20 STwin, FEI). The specific surface area $(S_{\mathrm{BET}})$ and pore size distribution are tested via ${\sf N}_{2}$ absorption–desorption isotherms, which are performed at liquid nitrogen temperature (77 K) on Micromeritics Tristar 3020 and ASAP 2020 Plus apparatus. The surface element composition and valence state of the photocatalysts are analyzed by $\mathsf{X}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, PHI 5300) with contaminant carbon $\mathrm{~\'{C}~}1s=284.6~\mathrm{eV})$ as a reference to calibrate the binding energies. The optical properties of the samples are recorded using an ultraviolet–visible diffuse reflectance spectrometer (Lambda 650S, PERKIN ELMER) equipped with an integration sphere and $\mathsf{B a S O_{4}}$ as reference. The photoluminescence (PL) spectra are obtained with a fluorescence spectrophotometer (Agilent, Cary Eclipse) at the emission wavelength of $420~\mathrm{{nm}}$ .
## 2.4. Photocatalytic hydrogen evolution tests
The photocatalytic hydrogen evolution tests are examined in a closed system with a gas recirculation. The light source is a 300 W Xe lamp (CEL-HXF-300), and the temperature is controlled at $20\ \mathrm{^\circC}$ by cooling circulating water. In detail, $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of photocatalyst is added into $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of deionized water containing ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf S O}_{3}$ $(0.25{\mathrm{~M}})$ and ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ $(0.35{\mathrm{~M}})$ as sacrificial agents. Then, $1\%$ Pt is added to the system as co-catalyst. Afterwards, the mixture is poured into the reactor and evacuated by a vacuum pump. The hydrogen evolution amount is determined by a gas chromatograph fitted with a $5\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ molecular sieve column and a thermal conductivity detector (TCD). Apparent quantum efficiency (AQE) is measured using different monochromatic light filter (320, 350, 400, 420, $450~\mathrm{nm}$ ) and is calculated according to the following equation:
$$
{\sf A Q E}\left(\%\right)=\frac{\mathrm{numberofreactedelectrons}}{\mathrm{numberofincidentphotons}}\times100\%
$$
$$
=\frac{\mathrm{numberofevolvedH_{2}m o l e c u l e s\times2}}{\mathrm{numberofincidentphotons}}\times100\%
$$
## 2.5. Photoelectrochemical tests
The photoelectrochemical measurements are carried out on an electrochemical workstation (Chenhua CHI760E, China) with a standard three-electrode system. The working electrodes are prepared as follows: $2~\mathrm{mg}$ of photocatalyst powder is dispersed in a mixed solution containing $400~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of water, $45~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of ethanol and $5~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of Nafion. Followed with ultrasound treatment for $30~\mathrm{min}$ the $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of resultant suspension is dropped and dried on a FTO substrate $(1\times1~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ . After being dried at room temperature, the FTO glass is used as the working electrode, and a Pt foil and a saturated calomel electrode (SCE) are used as the counter electrode and reference electrode, respectively. The photocurrent curves and Mott-Schottky curves are measured in a ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt S O}_{4}$ (0.5 M) aqueous solution, where a 300 W Xe lamp is used as light source. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) plots are detected in a mixed solution of potassium ferricyanide $(0.025\mathrm{~M})$ and muriate of potash $(0.1\ \mathrm{M})$ .
## 2.6. Computation method
The Vienna Ab Initio Package (VASP) [49,50] is employed to perform all the density functional theory (DFT) calculations within the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) using the PBE [51] formulation. The projected augmented wave (PAW) potentials [52,53] have been used to describe the ionic cores and take valence electrons into account using a plane wave basis set with a kinetic energy cutoff of $450~\mathrm{eV}$ . Partial occupancies of the Kohn-Sham orbitals are allowed using the Gaussian smearing method and a width of $0.05\ \mathrm{eV}.$ It is considered that electronic energy is selfconsistent when the energy change is smaller than $10^{-4}~\mathrm{eV}$ and the geometry optimization is considered convergent when the force change is smaller than $0.03\mathrm{eV}/{\mathring{\mathrm{A}}}.$ . Grimme’s DFT-D3 methodology [54] is used to describe the dispersion interactions. The Brillouin zone integral uses the surfaces structures of $2\times2\times1$ monkhorst pack K-point sampling. In particular, the adsorption energies $\left(\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{ads}}\right)$ are calculated as $\mathrm{E_{ads}=E_{a d/s u b}–E_{a d}–E_{s u b}}$ , and the $\operatorname{E}_{\mathbf{ad}/\cdot}$ - sub, $\mathtt{E}_{\mathrm{ad}}$ and ${\mathrm{E}}_{\mathrm{sub}}$ are the optimized adsorbate/substrate system, the adsorbate in the structure and the clean substrate respectively. The free energies are obtained by formula $\mathrm{G=E_{total}+E_{Z P E}–T S},$ and the
Etotal, $\mathtt{E}_{\mathrm{ZPE}}$ , and TS are the ground-state energy, zero-point energies, and entropy terms, respectively, with the latter two taking vibration frequencies from DFT.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Morphology and structure characterization
The synthesis process of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ can be illustrated in Fig. 1. Firstly, the In-MOF nanorods are synthesized by a typical method as described previously. Next, the tubular $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ is obtained by calcining In-MOF nanorods at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere, and the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the as-synthesized $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ is displayed in Fig. S1. The diffraction peaks coincide well with the cubic $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ (JCPDS: 060416). Afterwards, the calcination reactions are employed to synthesize $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ nanotubes with $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ and melamine as precursors. Finally, $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ heterostructure is formed via a facile hydrothermal method.
The morphology and microstructure of the composites are observed by scanning electron microscope (SEM) and fieldemission transmission electron microscopy (FE-TEM) analyses. The SEM images display the In-MOF nanorods and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanotubes, and the latter are synthesized by In-MOF pyrolysis (Fig. 2a and b). Compared to the In-MOF precursor, the tubular $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ is slightly curved, and its diameter is smaller, mainly due to the inhomogeneous shrinkage of microcrystalline during the pyrolysis process [44]. As Fig. 2c shows, after calcination reaction, the $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ composite maintains the tubular structure with a shorter length, implying the structure of the main catalyst is stable in the synthesis processes.
The FE-TEM images further demonstrate the tubular structure of $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{In}_{2}0_{3}@\operatorname{InN}}\end{array}$ composite (Fig. 3a and b). Meanwhile, the interface of the composite can be clearly seen in Fig. 3c. The lattice distance of 0.29 and $0.207{\mathrm{nm}}$ can be assigned to the (222) crystal plane of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ and (10 2) crystal plane of InN, further confirming the successful conversion of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ to InN crystal. Besides, the elemental mappings of the $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ imply that all elements are evenly distributed in the composite (Fig. 3d–i) and the atomic ratio is shown in Fig. S2. It should be noted that the N element is from the organic ligands of MOF and the added melamine. In addition, the irregular structure of the pristine $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ can be observed in Fig. S3.
As Fig. 4a–c shows, the FE-TEM images and enlarged TEM images of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ further corroborate that the layered $Z\mathrm{nIn}_{2}S_{4}$ is loaded on the surface of tubular $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ after the hydrothermal process. The co-existence of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ , InN and $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ can be clearly observed in Fig. 4d, which is circled with number 1, 2 and 3. The lattice distance of 0.29, 0.207 and $0.32~\mathrm{{nm}}$ can be attributed to the (2 2 2) crystal plane of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ , (1 0 2) crystal plane of InN and (222) crystal plane of $Z_{\mathrm{{nln}_{2}}}S_{4}$ , respectively, indicating the successful synthesis of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ ternary heterostructure. Additionally, as Fig. S4 shows, the elemental mappings of the ternary heterostructure further confirm the even distribution of thin-layered $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ on $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{In}_{2}0_{3}@\operatorname{InN}}\end{array}$ nanotubes.
Fig. 5a shows the XRD patterns of the $\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ with different mass of melamine as precursors. As for the pure $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ , the main diffraction peaks of $21.5^{\circ},30.6^{\circ},35.5^{\circ},51.0^{\circ}$ and $60.7^{\circ}$ are corresponded to the diffraction planes (211), (222), (400), (440) and (622) of cubic $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . Compared to pure $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , a new diffraction peak of $33.2^{\circ}$ appears after the calcination process obviously, which is assigned to the (101) crystal planes of hexagonal InN [46], confirming the partial conversion of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ to InN and the successful synthesis of $\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ . The reaction mechanism can be explained as follows. The ammonia gas from the decomposition of the melamine is firstly absorbed on the surface of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ nanotubes, then, the surficial $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ can react with ammonia to form
InN at $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [46]. Meanwhile, with the increasing amount of melamine added, the peak intensity of InN enhances, implying more $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ is converted to InN. As Fig. S5 shows, the calcination temperature also influences the mass ratio of InN in the composites. From the XRD patterns showed in Fig. 5b, it can be seen that the diffraction peaks of $21.6^{\circ}$ , $27.7^{\circ}$ , 47.2, $52.6^{\circ}$ and $55.6^{\circ}$ are corresponded to the diffraction planes (006), (102), (110), (116), (022) of hexagonal $Z\mathrm{nIn}_{2}S_{4}$ , suggesting the successful synthesis of the heterostructure. However, compared with $\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ , the diffraction peak intensity of $\mathrm{InN}$ at $33.2^{\circ}$ in the ternary heterostructure is not obvious (Fig. S6), as a result of the tiny amount and low diffraction intensity of InN.
The specific surface area $(S_{\mathrm{BET}})$ and pore size distribution of samples can be estimated by $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ absorption–desorption isotherms. As depicted in Fig. 6a, compared to pristine $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ $(76~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ , the $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ $(84~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ is slightly increased, which is mainly attributed to the gas-involved calcination process. As depicted in Fig. 6b, the pristine $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ has a relatively low $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ of $20.1~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . After coupling with $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ nanotubes, the ternary composite shows a significantly higher $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ of $158.1\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ , confirming the thin-layered $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ is well distributed on the $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{In}_{2}0_{3}@\operatorname{InN}}\end{array}$ nanotubes. This result implies the ternary photocatalyst with a high $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ could offer more active sites for water splitting. And the pore size distributions of samples are displayed in Fig. S7.
The Raman spectroscopy can analyze the carbon constituent of the photocatalysts. As displayed in Fig. 7a, $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ displays two prominent D and G bands, indicting the existence of partly graphitized carbon [55–57], which is formed in the pyrolysis process of In-MOF. At the same time, the binary and ternary photocatalysts display the two bands, demonstrating the partly graphitized carbon is stable in the calcination and hydrothermal processes, mainly because all the calcination processes are performed at an inert atmosphere. In addition, compared with $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , the $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value of $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{In}_{2}0_{3}@\operatorname{InN}}\end{array}$ is obviously increased, indicating the formation of defects upon the high temperature calcination process of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ .
The optical properties of the photocatalysts can be analyzed via UV–Vis. diffuse reflectance spectroscopy (UV–Vis. DRS), and the results shown in Fig. 7b demonstrate that the $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ has intense response in a wide light range, while the visible light absorption intensity of $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ is weak with an absorption edge at around $520~\mathrm{nm}$ . Moreover, the band gap energy of $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ is evaluated to be $2.25\ \mathrm{eV}$ by the Kubelka-Munk method (Fig. S8). Compared with $\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}S_{4}$ , the absorption edge wavelengths of the ternary photocatalyst decreases obviously, implying the light absorption capacity is not the decisive factor for the enhanced photocatalytic hydrogen evolution activity in this work. Furthermore, the visible light absorption intensity of the ternary photocatalysts is enhanced with the increasing mass ratio of InN, as shown in Fig. S9, and the calcination temperature also influences the optical properties of the ternary materials.
The surface chemical composition and chemical states of elements are investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). Fig. S10 reveals the existence of C, O, N, Zn, In, S elements in the photocatalysts. As Fig. 8a shows, the binding energies at 444.6 and $455.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to the In-O bond in $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ . In addition, the appeared two peaks at 443.5 and $451.2\mathrm{~\eV}$ can be ascribed to the In-N bond in $\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ . At the same time, the N 1s spectra of the $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ can be divided into two peaks at 398.8 and $400.8\ \mathrm{~eV},$ assigned to the lattice N and $_{\mathsf{N-C}}$ bond. Furthermore, the new peak at $397.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ is belonged to N-In bond in $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{In}_{2}0_{3}@\operatorname{InN}}\end{array}$ (Fig. 8b). Therefore, the results further confirm the formation of InN crystal in the calcination process. The binding energies located at 1021.5 and $1044.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ in the $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ (Fig. S11a). Moreover, the S 2p spectrum can be divided into two peaks at 161.9 and $162.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. S11c), which are attributed to $S^{2-}$ in the $Z\mathrm{nIn}_{2}S_{4}$ . Meanwhile, after coupling with $Z\mathrm{nIn}_{2}S_{4}$ layer, unapparent binding energy shifts of Zn 2p, S 2p, In 3d can be observed (Fig. S11), revealing the surface chemical composition of the as-obtained photocatalysts is stable. It should be noted that the peaks for In 3d in $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ and ternary composites are consistent with the $Z_{\mathrm{{nln}_{2}}}S_{4}$ , mainly because the $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ is located on the surface of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ or $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ nanotubes in the composites.
## 3.2. Photocatalytic activity test
The photocatalytic performance of the as-prepared samples can be tested with photocatalytic hydrogen evolution, using ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf S O}_{3}$ and ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ as scavengers under simulated sunlight irradiation. All hydrogen evolution activities are tested by four parallel experiments. Firstly, as Fig. 9a–c shows, by adjusting the mass ratio of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ and melamine precursors, as well as the temperature of the calcination, the $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{In}_{2}0_{3}@\operatorname{InN}}\end{array}$ has the highest hydrogen evolution rate when the mass ratio of the added $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ and melamine precursors is $1\colon20$ and the calcination temperature is at $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Then, the amount of $Z_{\mathrm{{nln}_{2}{S_{4}}}}$ loaded is further optimized (Fig. S12). Overall, according to the relationship of hydrogen evolution performance and the mass of catalysts (Fig. S13a), the ternary composite $\scriptstyle\ln_{2^{-}}$ $\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ reaches the optimum hydrogen evolution rate of $275\ \upmu\mathrm{mol/h}$ $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of catalyst), which is about 12.5 and 2.5 times folder than the pristine $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ $22\ \upmu\mathrm{mol/h})$ and $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ ( $112\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}/$ $\mathbf{h}_{.}^{\cdot}$ ), respectively. It is worthy to note that the highest rate is 1.8 times than the binary composite $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , suggesting the InN plays an important role in enhancing the photocatalytic hydrogen evolution activity. At the same time, the hydrogen evolution rate of the ternary composite prepared by hydrothermal method is obviously higher than the sample prepared by mechanical mixing the $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ and $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ (Fig. S14), demonstrating the importance of core–shell structure for hydrogen evolution. In addition, the optimum ternary $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ photocatalyst has a superior hydrogen evolution activity among MOF-derived $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ - based photocatalysts ever reported as Table S1 shows. For comparison, the hydrogen evolution activities of the optimal $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}/$ $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ photocatalyst in other sacrificial systems (methanol and lactic acid) are also tested. As shown in Fig. S15, the hydrogen evolution rates are obviously lower than that in $\Nu{\sf a}_{2}\sf S-N a_{2}\sf S O_{3}$ mixture, mainly because the photocatalytic system is more suitable for the weak alkaline environment [44]. The apparent quantum efficiencies (AQE) are further investigated under the same reaction conditions using specific incident wavelengths, and the AQE of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ is calculated as about $2.96\%$ at $350~\mathrm{\nm}$ (Fig. S13b). In addition, the stability is a vital property of photocatalysts. As illustrated in Fig. 9d, the hydrogen evolution rate is almost constant with the initial rate after five successive cycles. Moreover, the XRD pattern of the ternary composite after five cycles is nearly unchanged compared with the fresh photocatalyst, further indicating its excellent photocatalytic stability (Fig. S16).
## 3.3. Investigation of charge transfer efficiency
The photogenerated charges transfer efficiency is a vital factor affecting the hydrogen evolution performance of the photocatalysts. To further investigate the transfer and recombination behaviors of the photogenerated charges in the ternary composite, photocurrent responses, electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) and photoluminescence (PL) spectroscopy are performed. As illustrated in Fig. 10a, the PL emission intensity of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ is slightly weaker than the pristine $Z\mathrm{nIn}_{2}S_{4}$ , due to the reduced recombination of charge carriers. After embedding InN at the interface of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ heterostructure, the PL intensity decreases sharply, implying the InN can further accelerate the interfacial charge transfer between $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $Z\mathrm{nIn}_{2}S_{4}$ . As shown in Fig. 10b, the photocurrent response of the $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ ternary composite is significantly higher than the pristine materials and the binary samples, indicating the enhanced separation and transfer efficiency of photogenerated charges. Meanwhile, the EIS spectra shows the ternary photocatalyst has the smallest semicircle Nyquist plots (Fig. 10c), which demonstrates the $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2^{-}}$ $S_{4}$ has the optimal charges transfer performance compared to other counterparts, and this finding is consistent with PL and photocurrent responses results.
## 3.4. Theoretical calculation and possible photocatalytic mechanism
Furthermore, as depicted in Fig. S17, the flat bands of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ , $\scriptstyle\mathrm{In}_{2^{-}}$ $\mathrm{\Delta}0_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ and $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ are estimated using Mott-Schottky plots, which are $-0.69$ , $-0.98$ and 0.43 eV (vs SCE), respectively. As the electric potential of NHE is about $0.22\ \mathrm{eV}$ higher than SCE and the potential of the conduction band (CB) is $0.2\mathrm{eV}$ lower than flat band potential, the accurate CB of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ and $Z\mathrm{nIn}_{2}.$ - $S_{4}$ are predicted to be $-0.67$ , $-0.96$ and $-0.41$ eV (vs NHE). It should be noted that the CB of $\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ is about $0.3{\ \mathrm{eV}}$ lower than the pristine $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . As shown in Fig. S18, the valence band (VB) of materials can be analyzed by VB XPS spectrum, and the VB potential of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ is estimated as $1.81\ \mathrm{eV}$ according to the formula: $\mathrm{E_{NHE}}=\Phi+1.80–4.44$ ( $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{NHE}}$ : potential vs NHE; $\Phi\colon4.5\mathrm{~eV}$ , the electron work function of the analyzer) [58]. It is found that the VB potential of $\ln_{2}{\cal O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ is about $1.48\mathrm{eV}$ , obviously lower than that of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ . At the same time, the VB potential of $Z_{\mathrm{{nln}_{2}}}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ can be estimated as $1.48\ \mathrm{eV}$ with its CB potential and band gap energy, and the latter is obtained by the Kubelka-Munk method. As a consequence, the band structures of pristine samples can be illustrated in Fig. 10d.
In order to further explore the mechanism of the enhanced photocatalytic performance, a theoretical calculation is conducted based on density functional theory (DFT). As Fig. 11a–c shows, several possible schematic structure models of H absorption sites on photocatalysts are constructed. And the Gibbs free energy of these sites are calculated. Based on the previous report, the Gibbs free energy of an excellent photocatalyst should be close to zero. As depicted in Fig. 11d, compared with S atom and In atom site, the Gibbs free energy of Pt atom site on $\mathrm{Pt-In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\ @\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ is obviously closer to zero, which implies a smaller energy barrier for the photocatalytic hydrogen evolution reaction. On the other hand, this result confirms that the ternary photocatalyst system with Pt as co-catalyst has the lowest overpotential for the reaction, and the best photocatalytic hydrogen evolution performance.
Based on the above discussions, a possible photocatalytic hydrogen evolution mechanism can be proposed (Fig. 12). In the hierarchical structure, the excited carriers migrate from $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}@\mathrm{InN}$ to $Z\mathrm{nIn}_{2}S_{4}$ under the simulated sunlight irradiation, leading to reduced recombination of charge carriers. In particular, InN can adjust the surface composition and the band structure of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ , resulting in acceleration of the interfacial charges transfer between $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ and $Z\mathrm{nln}_{2}S_{4}$ , so as to greatly boost the photocatalytic performance.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we have successfully synthesized a novel $\ln_{2}{\cal O}_{3}@\vartheta$ - $\mathrm{InN}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}S_{4}$ ternary heterostructure by facile calcination and hydrothermal methods for the first time. The optimal ternary photocatalyst exhibits a superior hydrogen evolution rate of $275\upmu\mathrm{mol}/$ h $50\mathrm{mg}$ of catalyst) under simulated sunlight irradiation, which is so far an especially high value among several representative MOFderived $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -based photocatalysts for hydrogen evolution reported previously [38,40,43]. Various characterization technologies and DFT calculation have confirmed the fabrication and the superior photocatalytic performance of the ternary composite. In particular, the embedded InN not only adjusts the energy band of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ , but also accelerates the charge transfer at the interface of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ heterostructure, which can explain the superior performance of the ternary photocatalyst to the binary $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{ZnIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ composite reported recently [44]. This work might provide a novel strategy to boost the interfacial charge transfer in the heterostructure for enhanced photocatalytic hydrogen evolution performance. Further study on the exploration of the embedded InN in other kinds of composite photocatalysts are being underway.
